\section{Introduction}
Ujir (ISO 639-3 udj) is an Austronesian language of the northwestern Aru Islands, Maluku, Indonesia. It is spoken in the villages of Ujir and Samang by about 1,000 people (2011). Sources say children no longer acquire it and that one of the two villages has stopped using it. Malay is displacing it.
\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No educational materials were found.
\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia article on the Ujir language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ujir_language}
\item Endangered Languages Project entry: \url{https://endangeredlanguages.com/lang/2798}
\item VolkswagenStiftung project ``Roos \& ruins: A project to document the Ujir language of the Aru Islands'' (2013--2015): \url{https://projektdatenbank.volkswagenstiftung.de/projekt/0055817}
\item Numerals data page, Max Planck Institute for Evolutionary Anthropology: \url{https://lingweb.eva.mpg.de/channumerals/Ujir.htm}
\item Verbix page on Ujir: \url{https://docs.verbix.com/Languages/Ujir}
\end{itemize}
\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project profile of the Ujir. It contradicts linguistic sources in places, for example by calling the language an isolate, so it should be used with caution: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/index.php/people_groups/15685}
\end{itemize}
\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
The documentation project above may have produced archived recordings, but none were located in this search.
\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Ujir is endangered, with no known school use and no known pedagogical materials. Revitalisation work would depend on the documentation project's outputs. Contacting the project researchers is a possible next step, but no contacts were found here.
